{"id":"same-class-by-department","title":"Same job class, different department","tier":"CLEARED","question":"In large job classes used by more than one department, how far apart is the middle holder’s pay from one department to another once overtime is set aside?","finding":"115 job classes had 50 or more full-year holders in fiscal 2026 and 52 of them are used by two or more departments with ten holders each. In the typical one the best-paying department’s middle holder is paid 2.5% more than the lowest’s: the same class is paid much the same wherever it sits. 6 classes show a gap of a tenth or more. The widest is Attorney (Civil/Criminal) (class 8177): $282,075 in City Attorney against $219,122 in Public Defender (29%), then Automotive Service Worker (class 7410) (Municipal Transportation Agency over Police, 18%).","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2026: rows with at least nine-tenths of a full-time year’s paid hours. Pay is regular salary plus other pay; overtime is left out. For each class with 50 or more such holders, the middle value is taken in every department with ten or more, and the highest is set against the lowest. Board seats, elected offices and the Superior Court are left out. Ranked by the ratio. Titles are printed as the payroll prints them (Dep is deputy, Chf is chief, Asst is assistant, Sprv is supervisor, Ofc is officer, EMT is emergency medical technician).","limits":"Within one class, pay depends on the step reached, and so on length of service: a department with long-serving staff pays its middle holder more than one that has just hired. That is the likeliest reason for a gap such as the one between attorneys in different offices; the payroll does not record length of service. Premiums for shifts, sites and languages are part of other pay and differ by workplace. The table shows where a gap is, not that anyone is paid outside the scale.","columns":[{"k":"job","t":"Job class","type":"text"},{"k":"holders","t":"Full-year holders","type":"int"},{